how is that different from textbox
They are actually the same thing in this context!
In Drafter, Textbox is the component used to create a text input field.
For example:
Textbox(name="user_input", placeholder="Enter text here")
Textbox: Creates the input field.
name: Identifies the input so your program can access what the user typed.
placeholder: Displays a hint inside the empty field.
In short: A textbox is the actual UI component; a text input field is what that component creates.
You can use Textbox whenever you want users to type information into your Drafter application.




how do routes, page, and state work in drafter
In Drafter, routes, pages, and state work together to control how your web application is organized, what users see, and how your application remembers information.
Here's a simple breakdown:
1. Routes – Where the user goes
A route determines which page a user sees based on the URL they visit.
For example, imagine you're building a basketball shot calculator:
Route (URL)
What it displays
/
Home page
/calculator
Shot calculator
/about
About page

When someone clicks a button labeled "Start Calculator," the application navigates to /calculator.
Think of routes as directions to different parts of your website.
2. Pages – What the user sees
A page is the actual screen or interface displayed to the user.
For your basketball calculator, you might have:
Home Page: Introduction and instructions.
Calculator Page: Input fields for velocity, release height, launch angle, and distance.
Results Page: Displays whether the shot goes in, its trajectory, and other calculations.
Each page typically corresponds to a route.
Think of pages as the individual rooms in your website.
3. State – What your application remembers
State is information your application stores while someone is using it.
For example, on your basketball calculator, state could store:
Initial velocity: 8 m/s
Release height: 2 m
Launch angle: 45°
Distance from hoop: 5 m
Whether the user has submitted the calculation
When a user changes an input, the state updates. Your application can then use that updated information to calculate and display results.
Think of state as your application's temporary memory.
How they work together
Basketball calculator example
Route: /calculator
Determines which page opens
Page: Shot Calculator
Displays input fields and buttons
State: User Inputs
Stores velocity, angle, height, and distance
Calculation and Results
Uses state to determine whether the shot goes in
Quick comparison
Concept
Main purpose
Simple analogy
Routes
Navigate the app
Directions
Pages
Display content
Rooms
State
Store changing information
Memory

The easiest way to remember it:
Routes determine where you go.
Pages determine what you see.
State determines what information your app remembers.
